# CODEOWNERS for idfkit-conformance
#
# This repository holds the shared surface of two libraries: the conformance cases both
# run, the accepted divergences between them, and the governance files that fix the shared
# vocabulary. Nothing here belongs to one language, so nothing here merges on one
# language's say-so.
#
# FR-067: any change touching the shared surface, meaning public names, corpus cases, the
# parity record, or the register, requires review from a maintainer of the other language.
#
# FR-091: that requirement has NO OVERRIDE. There is no break-glass path, no "urgent fix"
# exemption, and no admin merge. If a reviewer from the other language is unavailable, the
# merge is blocked until one is available. This is deliberate: the two maintainer sets
# currently overlap, which makes the rule self-satisfying today, and that is precisely why
# it is written down now rather than the day they diverge.
#
# Enforcement note for repository administrators: CODEOWNERS only requests the review.
# The block comes from branch protection on the default branch, which must have "Require
# review from Code Owners" enabled and must NOT allow administrators to bypass it. Without
# both settings FR-091 is a comment rather than a gate.
#
# Team slugs: these must exist in the "idfkit" GitHub organization, and every listed team
# must have at least read access to this repository. GitHub silently ignores a CODEOWNERS
# entry naming a team that does not exist or cannot see the repository, which would turn
# the gate off without any error, so verify both after any org or team rename.

# Default: both languages review anything not matched more specifically below.
*                       @idfkit/python-maintainers @idfkit/javascript-maintainers

# The conformance cases. Both runners execute these, so a case that suits one language and
# not the other is a case that lands red on the other side.
/cases/                 @idfkit/python-maintainers @idfkit/javascript-maintainers
/manifest.json          @idfkit/python-maintainers @idfkit/javascript-maintainers

# The accepted-failure list. Adding a divergence excuses one side from an assertion the
# other still meets, which is not a decision one side makes alone.
/known-divergence.toml  @idfkit/python-maintainers @idfkit/javascript-maintainers

# The governance files: the naming register and the parity ledger. They define the words
# both libraries use in public. Both sides read them at a pinned governance tag, so a
# change here reaches both libraries at their next pin bump.
/governance/            @idfkit/python-maintainers @idfkit/javascript-maintainers

# The runners themselves, and the tooling that regenerates expected output. A change to
# how a case is compared changes what both gates mean.
/runners/               @idfkit/python-maintainers @idfkit/javascript-maintainers
/tools/                 @idfkit/python-maintainers @idfkit/javascript-maintainers

# This file, and the workflows that enforce the gates.
/.github/               @idfkit/python-maintainers @idfkit/javascript-maintainers
